{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miss: when the recent history is in a downtrend, the model reverts upward too quickly and predicts rebounds that do not occur. Add stronger mean-reversion *downward* (i.e., dampen rebounds) after sustained declines.",
    "Overshoot in weeks 3–4: the model frequently projects a late-horizon upswing (often back into low/mid-70s) while outcomes continue drifting lower/sideways (mid-60s). Cap late-horizon upside or apply a convex penalty to upward slopes after day ~15.",
    "Lag around sell-offs: during sharp drawdowns, the model underestimates the speed/magnitude of continued declines (it stays elevated while true prices keep falling). Increase downside momentum sensitivity for horizons 5–20 following a negative break.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: week 1 needs only mild adjustment; week 2 moderate downward correction; weeks 3–4 strongest correction (largest downward bias and most frequent false rebound)."
  ]
}
